d because i was  born and raised here  i feel very close to this part of town  i learned to swim in this corner over  there  when i was baby  long long time ago  the gondoliers profession looks back on  a long tradition starting in the 11th  century the city's well-to-do would have  themselves rolled around the city in  gondolas fitted with small cabins called  felsi  back when residents depended on gondolas  for transportation there were over ten  thousand on venice's canals  today only some 500 are left used mainly  by tourists  luciano pelicioli moores his gondola  beneath the famous rialto bridge one of  the most confident spots in venice  there's no shortage of tourists passing  by drumming up his business is part of  the gondola's job  good afternoon senora just 80 euros  80 euros will pay for a 45-minute  gondola ride the prices are standardized  according to strict guidelines  the licensed gondolieri have attended a  special school learned the city's  history and studied foreign languages  many people dream of becoming a  gondolier there are only 400 gondoliers  with a license  this profession immerses you completely  in this city  luciano pelecholi spends up to 14 hours  a day rolling his passengers through the  canals but he doesn't sing for them  amateur singers are no longer welcome on  the gondolas only professionals  well that brings us nicely to the end of  the show for today so thank you for  being with us and until next time take  care of yourselves and goodbye  you
 

